# Template only — no real values. Copy what you need into .env.local
# (gitignored). For a working local setup, `docs/LOCAL_DEV.md` gives the
# exact file to write and where each value comes from.

# Mock provider chaos flags — all default to true (see lib/mock-provider/chaos.ts).
# Set to "false" or "0" to disable a specific flag deploy-wide; per-request
# query param overrides (?duplicates=false) win over these.
# CHAOS_DUPLICATES=true
# CHAOS_SCHEMA_DRIFT=true
# CHAOS_NULL_FIELDS=true
# CHAOS_RATE_LIMIT=true
# CHAOS_SERVER_ERROR=true
# CHAOS_EXPIRED_TOKEN=true
# CHAOS_FUTURE_DATES=true

# Deterministic seed for the mock provider's dataset — same seed,
# same failure sequence.
# MOCK_PROVIDER_SEED=42

# Stage 2 (Ingestion & Transform) — server-only, never exposed to the
# browser (lib/supabase/service-client.ts). Get these from the Supabase
# project dashboard (Project Settings > API); real values go in
# .env.local, never here.
# SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# Base URL the ingestion route calls to reach the mock provider. Defaults
# to the request's own origin (self-fetch, same Next.js app) — only set
# this if that detection is unreliable in a given deploy target.
# MOCK_PROVIDER_BASE_URL=

# Shared secret the provider-webhook Edge Function checks on inbound
# calls (a header, not a body field — see supabase/functions/provider-webhook).
# This is the caller's copy. The function's own copy lives in supabase/.env,
# which `supabase start` hands to the Edge Functions container through
# config.toml's [edge_runtime.secrets]; the two must match.
# WEBHOOK_SHARED_SECRET=

# Shared secret POST /api/ingestion/run requires in an x-ingestion-secret
# header. The route writes with the service-role client and takes org_id
# from its body, so without this any caller who guesses an orgs.id could
# write into that tenant. Unset means the route rejects everything.
# INGESTION_TRIGGER_SECRET=

# Stage 4 (Dashboard) — these two DO reach the browser bundle, unlike
# everything above. The anon key is public by design: it only ever gets a
# request as far as Postgres, where RLS decides what comes back (ADR 0007).
# The URL must be reachable from outside any container, because the browser
# is what uses it — the server-side value is SUPABASE_URL above, which
# compose.yaml overrides to the in-network gateway.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Stage 5 (RAG & Agent) — shared secret the `embed` Edge Function checks on
# inbound calls. Two copies, like the webhook secret above: this one is the
# caller's (the indexer and the agent), and the function's own copy lives in
# supabase/.env, which `supabase start` hands to the Edge Functions container
# through config.toml's [edge_runtime.secrets]. Unset means the function
# rejects everything.
# EMBED_SHARED_SECRET=

# Stage 5 (RAG & Agent) — which model the copilot runs on.
#
# Set exactly one of the keys below and the route works. All of them are read
# server-side only, in app/api/agent/chat and lib/agent/providers, never in a
# component and never in the browser bundle. With none set the route answers
# 503 and says so, rather than failing inside a client.
#
# The agent's safety properties do not come from the vendor (ADR 0009): RLS
# scopes what a tool can read, the registry bounds what a tool can do, and
# citations are checked against what a tool actually returned. Answer *quality*
# does vary — point LLM_PROVIDER at one and run `task evals` to see how much.

# Anthropic. Used first when several are configured.
# ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5

# Groq — free tier, OpenAI-compatible. https://console.groq.com/keys
# The model must support tool calling; an agent whose model cannot call a
# function has no way to reach any data. Catalogues change — a 404 means the
# default below is gone, and
#   curl https://api.groq.com/openai/v1/models -H "Authorization: Bearer $GROQ_API_KEY"
# lists what the account can actually reach.
#
# Watch the free tier's tokens-per-minute limit. This agent resends its system
# prompt and four tool schemas on every step, which is ~3.5k input tokens per
# call, so a two-step turn is most of a minute's budget. A *reasoning* model
# makes that much worse: qwen3.6-27b spent 4,672 output tokens on one answer,
# nearly all of it thinking that gets discarded. Prefer a non-reasoning model
# here.
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# GROQ_MODEL=openai/gpt-oss-20b

# NVIDIA NIM — free tier, OpenAI-compatible. https://build.nvidia.com
# Unverified: no NVIDIA key exists in this development environment, so unlike
# the Groq path this one has never been called. Set NVIDIA_MODEL if it 404s.
# NVIDIA_API_KEY=
# NVIDIA_MODEL=meta/llama-3.3-70b-instruct

# Anything else that speaks /chat/completions: Together, OpenRouter, a local
# vLLM or Ollama. Needs its own base URL, so it is never picked by accident.
# LLM_API_KEY=
# LLM_BASE_URL=http://localhost:11434/v1
# LLM_MODEL=

# Pin one explicitly. Without it the first configured provider above wins;
# with it, a missing key is an error rather than a silent fallback to another
# provider (and another bill).
# LLM_PROVIDER=groq
